hers\u003c/strong\u003e where everyone has opinions about the music and no one wants to be DJ all night.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHow it plays:\u003c/strong\u003e the host starts a session on their own device (phone, laptop, whatever's hooked up to the speaker) and it shows a join code and a \u003cstrong\u003eQR code\u003c/strong\u003e. Guests scan it with their phone camera — no app to install, it just opens in their mobile browser — type a name, and they're in.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFrom there guests search a music catalog and queue up tracks they want played. Everyone in the room can vote on the queued tracks, and votes reorder what's coming  up next live — so the crowd, not any one person, decides what plays. Once your queued track plays, your slot opens back up so you can queue something new.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe host's device is the one actually playing the music — either full playback through \u003cstrong\u003eApple Music\u003c/strong\u003e, or a free mode with no login where the winning track just deep-links out to whatever you normally use. Everything guest-side is mobile browser only, nothing to download.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt's a \u003cstrong\u003eJava\u003c/strong\u003e app backed by \u003cstrong\u003eFirestore\u003c/strong\u003e on embedded \u003cstrong\u003eJetty\u003c/strong\u003e, deployed on \u003cstrong\u003eCloud Run\u003c/strong\u003e, with three playback backends (\u003cstrong\u003eiTunes jukebox\u003c/strong\u003e, \u003cstrong\u003eSpotify\u003c/strong\u003e, \u003cstrong\u003eApple Music\u003c/strong\u003e) behind one interface. Spotify's locked to invited accounts only right now — \u003cstrong\u003eSpotify\u003c/strong\u003e hasn't approved the app for public API access yet. Happy to talk through the stack or just the gameplay here.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"Built this for house **parties/get-togethers** where everyone has opinions about the music and no one wants to be DJ all night.                                         \r\n\r\n**How it plays:** the host starts a session on their own device (phone, laptop, whatever's hooked up to the speaker) and it shows a join code and a **QR code**. Guests scan it with their phone camera — no app to install, it just opens in their mobile browser — type a name, and they're in.                                                \r\n\r\nFrom there guests search a music catalog and queue up tracks they want played. Everyone in the room can vote on the queued tracks, and votes reorder what's coming  up next live — so the crowd, not any one person, decides what plays. Once your queued track plays, your slot opens back up so you can queue something new.          \r\n \r\nThe host's device is the one actually playing the music — either full playback through **Apple Music**, or a free mode with no login where the winning track just deep-links out to whatever you normally use. Everything guest-side is mobile browser only, nothing to download.                                                \r\n \r\nIt's a **Java** app backed by **Firestore** on embedded **Jetty**, deployed on **Cloud Run**, with three playback backends (**iTunes jukebox**, **Spotify**, **Apple Music**) behind one interface. Spotify's locked to invited accounts only right now — **Spotify** hasn't approved the app for public API access yet. Happy to talk through the stack or just the gameplay here.","submitter_user":"skavanagh","user_is_author":true,"tags":["games","mobile","show"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/cshefz","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/cshefz/aux_control_let_whole_room_vote_on_music"},{"short_id":"3he4yj","created_at":"2026-07-27T11:40:05.739-05:00","title":"Finding bugs in Raft implementations","url":"https://antithesis.com/blog/2026/finding-bugs-in-raft-implementations/","score":23,"flags":0,"comment_count":3,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"eatonphil","user_is_author":false,"tags":["distributed","testing"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/3he4yj","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/3he4yj/finding_bugs_raft_implementations"},{"short_id":"pngdot","created_at":"2026-07-27T12:40:23.753-05:00","title":"Advantages and disadvantages of Windows NT 3.1","url":"https://dfarq.homeip.net/advantages-and-disadvantages-of-windows-nt-3-1/","score":19,"flags":0,"comment_count":4,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"raymii","user_is_author":false,"tags":["historical","windows"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/pngdot","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/pngdot/advantages_disadvantages_windows_nt_3_1"},{"short_id":"mv8t4p","created_at":"2026-07-27T11:35:19.922-05:00","title":"Quick \u0026 Easy Parser Combinators","url":"https://www.cyan.sh/blog/posts/tutorial-quick-easy-parser-combinators.html","score":20,"flags":0,"comment_count":6,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"abnercoimbre","user_is_author":false,"tags":["programming"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/mv8t4p","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/mv8t4p/quick_easy_parser_combinators"}]